   126	### 2.2 The leading-form data, and the two points at infinity
   127	
   128	`[l(P), l(Q)] = 0` for the top forms, so `l(P) = alpha H^d`, `l(Q) = beta H^e` with
   129	`gcd(d,e) = 1`, `deg H = K`, `deg P = Kd`, `deg Q = Ke` (proved in MKS §2.1 and
   130	re-proved here in one line; the statement is classical and is *not* carried from an
   131	ABSENT source). Degree-minimality forces `d, e >= 2` and `d != e`, hence
   132	`max(d,e) >= 3` and `D = K max(d,e) >= 3K` (GGV Rem 4.2, `gv.txt:718`).
   133	
   134	> **THEOREM NU-TWO (VERIFIED in `refs/`, gauge-free).** In the gauge (GEN), for a
   135	> degree-minimal counterexample,
   136	> ```text
   137	>     H = L_1^{u} L_2^{v} ,   L_1, L_2 distinct linear forms,  u + v = K,  u != v,
   138	>     nu := #{distinct roots of H} = 2 ,     M_s = n - 2 .
   139	> ```
   140	> *Source, firsthand.* Moh **Prop 4.5** (`moh.txt:1594-1597`): the top forms of `g`
   141	> and of the `T_i` "are powers of a common form which has **at most two distinct
   142	> linear factors**. Moreover the multiplicities for the two factors are
   143	> **different**" — the preceding remark (`1589-1591`) reads this as "at most two
   144	> points at infinity for `f(x,y)=0`". **Lemma 5.3** (`2453-2457`): the smallest disc
   145	> containing all roots of `g T_1` has logarithmic radius `-1` **iff** `M_s = n-2`
   146	> **and** the top form of `g` has **two** roots. **Prop 5.4** (`2321-2330`): if that
   147	> radius is `> -1` then either `k[x,y] = k[f,g]` (invertible) or an automorphism
   148	> lowers `deg f` and `deg g` simultaneously (non-minimal). ∎
   149	
   150	**This closes `OPEN[NU2-RIGIDITY]` (MKS §8) POSITIVE.** MKS proved `nu = 2` only in
   151	(SUBRECT); Moh's Prop 4.5 gives it in (GEN), a *linear* gauge, so `nu = 2` is an
